---
import DocsLayout from "../../layouts/DocsLayout.astro";
import CodeBlock from "../../components/CodeBlock.astro";

const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL.replace(/\/$/, "");
---

<DocsLayout
  currentSlug="parity"
  title="Parity harness"
  description="How Corvene is kept identical to GitHub Desktop 3.6.6, and how its deliberate differences are tracked."
>
  <h2 id="goal">The goal</h2>
  <p>
    Corvene is a port, not a reimagining. Layout, colours, wording, shortcuts, dialogs, foldouts and menus follow GitHub
    Desktop 3.6.6, down to the pixel on macOS. Where Corvene improves on it, the improvement is a
    <a href={`${base}/docs/flags`}>flag</a> that can be switched off, and the <em>GitHub Desktop</em> preset switches them all off.
  </p>

  <h2 id="harness">The harness</h2>
  <p>
    <code>tools/parity</code> drives both applications through the same scenarios and diffs what they draw:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      A private copy of GitHub Desktop runs under the Chrome DevTools Protocol with a fresh profile; Corvene runs with
      <code>CORVENE_CONTROL=&lt;port&gt;</code> and the <code>snapshots</code> feature, which lets the harness click, type and
      capture the window off screen.
    </li>
    <li>
      Both get a freshly built fixture repository with fixed authors and dates, so the two copies have identical commit
      ids, and fake signed-in accounts, since neither app can sign in during a run.
    </li>
    <li>
      Every scenario is a list of steps (click, type, press, open a dialog, snap). After every <code>snap</code> both
      windows are captured at 1367×814 in the dark and the light theme and compared pixel by pixel, with a tolerance and
      optional masks per snap.
    </li>
    <li>
      The report in <code>target/parity/latest/index.html</code> shows each pair as an overlay, side by side, swipe and
      blink. The run fails when any snap exceeds its threshold.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <CodeBlock code={"cargo build -p corvene --features snapshots\npython3 tools/parity/parity.py [scenario…]"} />
  <p>
    Corvene runs under <code>CORVENE_FLAGS=preset=github-desktop</code> during a parity run. A touched view's scenarios may
    not get worse, and every new surface or state gets a scenario.
  </p>

  <h2 id="deviations">Deliberate differences</h2>
  <p>
    Every difference is recorded with the GitHub Desktop source file it departs from, and when it can be switched off it is
    a flag. A few examples:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li><code>202-fs-watcher</code>: the repository refreshes when files change, not only on window focus.</li>
    <li><code>201-commit-templates</code>: the repository's <code>commit.template</code> prefills the description.</li>
    <li><code>102-smooth-wheel-scrolling</code>: mouse-wheel ticks animate instead of jumping 40 px.</li>
    <li><code>206-import-from-github-desktop</code>: File › Import Repositories from GitHub Desktop…</li>
    <li><code>105-tree-sitter-highlighting</code>: highlighting for languages GitHub Desktop leaves plain (off by default).</li>
    <li>
      <strong>Bug fixes</strong>, tagged as such in the registry, fix behaviour GitHub Desktop plainly gets wrong (a selected
      row losing its colour under the pointer, a stale warning in Add Local Repository). They too can be switched back.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    Not flagged, because there is nothing to switch: no telemetry or crash reporting of any kind, and the native engine
    itself.
  </p>

  <h2 id="linux">Linux and Windows</h2>
  <p>
    GitHub Desktop has no Linux release, so Linux Corvene follows the community build's menus and wording. The Linux and
    Windows chrome (title bar, menu bar, window controls) is newer than the macOS one and not yet pixel-identical
    everywhere; expect small differences.
  </p>
</DocsLayout>
